Is my previous random grab bag of sustaino-design related things still a usable rough set of guideposts to build a conversation from?

- what are [[signs signaling sustainability|Signs Signaling Sustainability]]? — how does a project build in little flags declaring some climate change related bit or piece?
- does a project have to fully embody all your sustainable/earth-centered/climate-protecting ideals, or if it shows an alternative path, delivers a little bit of a push in the right direction, is that okay?
- how can we do our design signaling in more ephemeral ways: not better materials (this is typically what you get when you search for "sustainable graphic design"), but entirely different frameworks and processes that might deliver a message or result but without matter/energy expensive forms?
- one must define what it is they are sustaining; you have to know what you want to preserve or maintain or hold up. So "sustainable design" in status quo parlance is probably design for all the people and things we would have been designing for anyway, but with "better" materials or processes or working conditions; but doesn't necessarily ask a question about if those companies or systems the design is for are worth sustaining...
- are there other words or principles or whatever that make more sense or are clearer or draw from other ethics or ideals?
- Is this a place where speculative or critical design becomes really useful? how do we design for these other alternative realities where everything is restorative? where everything is earth-centered (climate-centered? plant-centered? planet-centered? nature-centered? universe-centered)? where political-economies factor find value in all our intertwined ecologies?
- free/libre software & cybernetics & alternative tools for making,
- being a farmer instead of a designer (actually I just see farming as part of my designing)
- collaborating with some other designers and design educators on this "[[Climate Designers]]" meetup group / goal: reframe designing as a climate-change fighting profession.
- What about climate change do designers need to know? how does that get integrated into all aspects of the design discipline (including becoming central to design education)?
- How can you help draw down carbon with your designs?
- Facts and pragmatics.
- Talking to climate scientists (talking to anyone doing anything climate related really)
- do we just become politicians &/or activists?
- Is designing just a service to all these others doing the "real" work? can we do some of the heavy lifting, or are we stuck in the role of service?
